{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And Sarah died in Kiryat Arba — that is, Hebron.</b> In the Yalkut it is concluded that this place had four names, and they are: Eshkol, Mamrei, Kiryat Arba, Hebron. And I shall venture to give a good reason and understanding for these four names, for this place was designated for graves; therefore it was called by four names that allude to four manners in which death is found among them. For there is a death in which a person dies for his own sin, as it is written (Numbers 27:3), ״כִּי בְחֶטְאוֹ מֵת״. And there is a death on account of the sin of others — like sons who are seized for the sin of their father, and a righteous man seized for the sin of his generation. And there are those who ״without iniquity run from the world״ — from the side of natural death and the separation of the four elements, for it is a natural thing that everything composite ultimately comes apart. And there is a death of kissing — this is the death of the righteous, who die by a kiss — and it is an expression of joining (חִבּוּר); its meaning is that when their thought and soul ascend and cleave on high to the Active Intellect, once they have ascended they never descend, for their soul remains joined there." "And corresponding to them these four names came, which follow these kinds of death: מַמְרֵא — corresponding to the one who dies for his own sin, after the expression ״מַמְרִים הֱיִיתֶם עִם ה׳״ and the expression ״זָקֵן מַמְרֵא״. אֶשְׁכּוֹל — corresponding to the one who dies on account of the sins of others who are made childless (שַׁכּוּלִים) on his account, for one who buries his children is called שַׁכּוּל, as it is stated (Genesis 27:45), ״לָמָה אֶשְׁכַּל גַּם שְׁנֵיכֶם יוֹם אֶחָד״. For this sinner and rebel is left childless and bereft because of that one who was put to death for his sin; therefore they called that place ״אֶשְׁכּוֹל״, on account of the bereavement (שִּׁכּוּל) which they cut off from the world through the sin of that one. קִרְיַת אַרְבַּע — after natural death, the separation of the four elements, for man is a small city, a קִרְיַת אַרְבַּע. חֶבְרוֹן — after joining (חִבּוּר) and the death of kissing. And the death of the righteous Sarah was not from the side of the first two kinds, in which there is an aspect of sin, but from the side of the two kinds in which ״without iniquity they run״. Therefore it says ״וַתָּמָת שָׂרָה בְּקִרְיַת אַרְבַּע הִוא חֶבְרוֹן״ — it mentioned only these two names, because her death was of their kind — that is, through the separation of the four elements and by a kiss and joining, as stated." "<b>And Abraham came to mourn for Sarah and to weep for her.</b> The word ״לְשָׂרָה״ he inserted between the joined verbs, for it should have said ״לִסְפֹּד וְלִבְכּוֹת לְשָׂרָה״. Moreover, all of it is superfluous, for it should have said ״לְסָפְדָהּ וְלִבְכּוֹתָהּ״. Also, in every other place weeping precedes eulogizing, whereas here the order is reversed — for everywhere there are three days for weeping and seven for eulogizing (Mo'ed Katan 27a). And it appears that in the case of every other deceased person, the mourning goes and diminishes, for as days multiply everything is forgotten. But this righteous woman — every single day they felt yet more keenly her absence, on account of the loss of her Torah and her good deeds that were lost to her generation. Therefore he put eulogizing before weeping. And our Rabbis of blessed memory further said (Sanhedrin 22a), ״Whoever's first wife dies, it is as though the Temple was destroyed in his days,״ etc. Therefore he first came to eulogize for Sarah — on her own account — but he did not weep over her, for he knew that she had ascended on high to a place more precious and honored than that in which she had been in this land of darkness (after Jeremiah 31:9 and context). And afterward he wept over the Temple that was destroyed in his days, for even the angels of peace weep bitterly over its destruction, and so they expounded concerning him the verse ״וְדָמֹעַ תִּדְמַע עֵינִי״ etc. (Jeremiah 13:17). And therefore he interrupted with the word ״לְשָׂרָה״, because the weeping was over what seemed to him as though the Temple, which stood a hundred cubits high, was destroyed in his days. Therefore the allusion comes through a small כָּף, for כָּף equals one hundred, and it means to say: as though those hundred cubits were diminished and became small. And this is a precious allusion." "And in the Gemara of Sanhedrin (46a) it was raised as a question: is eulogizing a term for the living or for the dead? And the conclusion is that eulogizing is a term for the dead. Therefore it says ״לִסְפּוֹד לְשָׂרָה״, to indicate that the eulogizing — which is a term of hers, that is, of the dead — was for her. Another matter: he mentioned ״לְשָׂרָה״ to tell you that for her own self he did not eulogize, for he knew that only the setting of her sun was in this world, but she eats of the terumah of the fruit of her deeds in the World to Come; rather, he eulogized over her name — for she was שָׂרָה (ruler) over all the world in her wisdom, and woe to her generation, for they lost this sovereignty from the side of her wisdom and her deeds. But to weep for her, he wept a small weeping, for since she was righteous she was satisfied with a palmful of small contentment. Therefore they eat in this world and inherit the World to Come, and consequently they are not included in the punishment of the sling-stone's hollow, as it is written (I Samuel 25:29), ״וְהָיְתָה נֶפֶשׁ אֲדֹנִי צְרוּרָה בִּצְרוֹר הַחַיִּים״ etc., ״וְאֵת נֶפֶשׁ אֹיְבֶיךָ יְקַלְּעֶנָּה בְּתוֹךְ כַּף הַקָּלַע״ — that is, in Gehennom." "Another matter: he alluded through the small כָּף that she died through the venom of the serpent, which brought death to man, who had been created from one end of the heavens to the other; and through the sin of the serpent he was diminished and set at a hundred cubits, as it is stated (Psalms 139:5), ״וַתָּשֶׁת עָלַי כַּפֶּכָה״ — כָּף is one hundred. And because he was diminished and set at the number כָּף, from then on his strength was weakened and he became ready for death."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {2 "The small kaf (כ״ף קְטַנָּה) refers to the letter ך at the end of וְלִבְכֹּתָהּ, the final form of kaf being called the 'bent' or 'small' kaf; its numerical value is 20, but the word כַּף in its full spelling equals 100.", 4 "The wordplay depends on כַּפֶכָה (your palm) in Psalms 139:5 being read through Aramaic translation tradition as related to diminution; the numerical value of כָּף is 20, but כַּף in the full spelling used for her赎 (atonement/ה׳) equals 100, matching the 100-cubit height alluded to earlier."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:29:47.013121677Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}